**`ErrorBoundary.tsx`** — drop-in class component since error boundaries require lifecycle methods unavailable in hooks.

**Usage:**

```tsx
// Default fallback UI
<ErrorBoundary>
  <MyComponent />
</ErrorBoundary>

// Custom fallback
<ErrorBoundary fallback={(error, retry) => (
  <div>
    <p>Oops: {error.message}</p>
    <button onClick={retry}>Try again</button>
  </div>
)}>
  <MyComponent />
</ErrorBoundary>
```

**Key details:**
- `getDerivedStateFromError` captures the error and triggers the fallback render
- `componentDidCatch` logs both the error and the React component stack to the console
- `retry` resets state to `null`, which re-mounts the child tree
- The `fallback` prop is optional — omit it to use the built-in UI
- `role="alert"` makes the fallback accessible to screen readers
